Subject: Register bundles: one wire format, independent of QJS_AOT

What this does
  Makes a register bundle's on-disk and in-memory layout depend on
  `QJS_REGVM` alone. Every `QJS_REGVM && !QJS_AOT` / `!QJS_REGVM || QJS_AOT`
  guard on the serialize, deserialize and in-memory-layout paths becomes a
  plain `QJS_REGVM` guard, and the AOT-only "exec code inline in the header"
  form is removed.

Why we need it
  The register bundle had two formats, chosen by whether the *build* that wrote
  it had AOT enabled: a non-AOT build wrote a compact wire program in the lazy
  region and no stack bytecode; an AOT build inlined the decoded exec code in
  the function header and kept a stack bytecode. A bundle written by one could
  therefore not be read by the other.

  That is not hypothetical. The three checked-in `builtin-*.regvm.h` payloads
  (`Array.fromAsync`, `Iterator.zip`, `Iterator.zipKeyed`) are written by a
  non-AOT register `qjsc` and compiled into every register build, including AOT
  ones. An AOT build read their header as an inline exec program, copied wire
  bytes into the exec buffer, failed validation, and the auto-init property
  consequently resolved to `JS_UNINITIALIZED`:

      $ printf 'print(typeof Array.fromAsync);\n' | ./aot-build
      uncaught: [uninitialized]
      Assertion 'list_empty(&rt->gc_obj_list)' failed at teardown

  Every retry of the auto-init rebuilt its argument objects and leaked them,
  which is the teardown assertion. The interpreter and a non-AOT register build
  were both fine, so the fault was invisible until something read one of those
  three properties.

What it adds
  One format. The writer always emits the register program in wire form in the
  lazy region and always records `stack_size`, `byte_code_len` and `ic_count` as
  zero for a register build; the reader always decodes from the wire, always
  bounds the decoded length at 16x the wire (it is never smaller than 1/16), and
  never walks a stack program for object-literal template metadata. The
  in-memory layout follows: no `byte_code_buf` and no `js_scan_argv_safe` for a
  register build, AOT or not.

  `QJS_AOT` now changes only how a function is *entered* and where the profile
  goes, which is what it was for.

Correctness
  `Array.fromAsync` and `Iterator.zipKeyed` read as `function` in an AOT build,
  repeated reads included, with no leak. The op suite (`./m1.sh e2ops`, 399
  upstream kernels in 21 files) went from 19/21 to 21/21; the two failures were
  exactly the files that read those properties. `e2k` 19/19, `fast` GOAL MET,
  and the eight Octane benchmarks unchanged or better (richards 3.20x,
  deltablue 2.97x).

Bytecode version
  The bundle format changes shape, so a bundle written by an older build is not
  readable by this one. The format was already rejected by a version match, and
  the three checked-in payloads are regenerated from source, so no bump is
  needed beyond the one 9999 already carries.

